    "text": "**Case A**\n\n- **A1 \u2013 Cannot tell.** The search covered only the past seven days, so earlier misuse of the key is not ruled out, and the burst itself is not yet confirmed as misuse.\n- **A2 \u2013 Yes.** The burst and the payout changes happened at 19 minutes and the check scored at 30 minutes without flagging. This assumes the check could see request and account-change data, which the case does not state; if it could not, the answer would be \"Cannot tell\".\n- **A3 \u2013 No, not by itself.** A shared payout destination is a link worth following, but it could also be a shared processor, an agent, or a mule account.\n- **A4 \u2013 Yes, weakly.** Thirty days is long enough that a victim of diverted payouts would often have complained, so the silence counts a little towards legitimacy, but reports can lag and nobody may have looked.\n- **A5 \u2013 Yes.** A new network path and fingerprint minutes after publication, combined with a payout destination change and a faster schedule, is the classic pattern of key theft.\n\n**Case B**\n\n- **B1 \u2013 Yes.** D has 0 of 100 against 2 of 100 for each of the others, on the same denominator, though the difference is small in statistical terms.\n- **B2 \u2013 No.** A combined 5 percent is an average, so one account can sit above it while another sits below.\n- **B3 \u2013 Yes.** The search covered all merchant accounts and found only A and the closed account. It can only show accounts under that person's recorded identity, not ones held through nominees.\n- **B4 \u2013 Cannot tell.** Different analysts and days does not establish independence; they may have looked at the same evidence, or the second may have read the first note.\n- **B5 \u2013 No.** A blocked payment never completed, so it could not generate a chargeback; the absence of one says nothing about whether it was fraud.\n- **B6 \u2013 Yes.** Shared templates, a common descriptor pattern, settlement through the same four accounts and the link to a rejected application are enough to justify looking at them together.\n\nRESULT CF-050 | A1: Cannot tell | A2: Yes | A3: No, not by itself | A4: Yes, weakly | A5: Yes | B1: Yes | B2: No | B3: Yes | B4: Cannot tell | B5: No | B6: Yes"
